[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding Polymorphism: Compile-time vs Runtime"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Polymorphism is a core concept in Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) that allows a single interface to represent different underlying forms (data types). The word originates from Greek, meaning "many forms." In software engineering, polymorphism drastically improves code maintainability, extensibility, and reusability."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Two Pillars of Polymorphism"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Polymorphism is broadly categorized into two main types based on when the decision of which method to execute is made:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Compile-time Polymorphism (Static Binding / Early Binding)"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Runtime Polymorphism (Dynamic Binding / Late Binding)"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Compile-time Polymorphism"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Compile-time polymorphism is resolved during the compilation phase. The compiler determines which method or function to call based on the method signature (the method name and its parameters). The most common implementation of compile-time polymorphism is Method Overloading."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"class MathOperations {
    // Overloaded methods with different parameters
    public int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    public int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Runtime Polymorphism"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Runtime polymorphism is resolved during program execution, rather than by the compiler. This is achieved through Method Overriding and inheritance. When a subclass provides a specific implementation of a method that is already defined in its parent class, the JVM or runtime environment decides which method to invoke based on the actual object type at runtime."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"class Animal {
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("The animal makes a sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("The dog barks");
    }
}

class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("The cat meows");
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"When utilizing runtime polymorphism, a parent class reference variable can point to a child class object, enabling dynamic method dispatch."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal myAnimal;

        myAnimal = new Dog();
        myAnimal.makeSound(); // Outputs: The dog barks

        myAnimal = new Cat();
        myAnimal.makeSound(); // Outputs: The cat meows
    }
}"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences Summary"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Binding Time: Compile-time polymorphism resolves during compilation; runtime polymorphism resolves during execution."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mechanism: Achieved via Method Overloading (Compile-time) vs Method Overriding (Runtime)."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Performance: Compile-time polymorphism is slightly faster because method calls are bound statically. Runtime polymorphism introduces minor overhead due to dynamic method dispatch lookup."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Flexibility: Runtime polymorphism offers higher flexibility, allowing systems to be easily extended with new classes without altering existing client code."}]
